<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    Nop.Admin - the administration UI, and the LARGEST SINGLE UI SURFACE in the
    application: 277 .cs files and 325 .cshtml views (design section 6).
    Downstream of Nop.Core, Nop.Data, Nop.Services and Nop.Web.Framework, and a
    parallelizable SIBLING of Nop.Web - neither project references the other, and there
    is no ProjectReference between them in either direction.
    (Requirements 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.5, 2.4, 4.11, 5.7, 5.10)

    SCOPE OF TASK 8.1: this file, the package graph and the project references ONLY.
    No .cs file, no .cshtml view, no AdminAreaRegistration.cs and no Web.config was
    touched - those are tasks 8.2 (area routing), 8.3 (controllers), 8.4 (views),
    8.5 (static assets), 8.6 (ImageSharp) and 8.7 (configuration / obsolete files).
    The project is EXPECTED not to compile at the end of 8.1; the surviving CS*/RZ*
    errors are the inventory those tasks consume, and 8.8 is the gate.

    ===================================================================================
    WHY Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web
    ===================================================================================
    Like Nop.Web and unlike Nop.Web.Framework (which has zero .cshtml files and stays on
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk - see the long note in that project), Nop.Admin owns 325 .cshtml
    views under Views\. The Web SDK imports the Razor SDK and supplies the ASP.NET Core
    app targets.

    NOTE: there is deliberately NO explicit <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    below. Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web adds it implicitly and restating it fails the build with
    NETSDK1086 - the same trap task 7.1 recorded for Nop.Web. This is the one place the
    Nop.Web.Framework pattern must NOT be copied.

    THE TFM IS THE OS-AGNOSTIC net10.0, NOT net10.0-windows - and that is a deliberate
    decision with a cost attached, because this project is the ONLY one in the solution
    that genuinely binds the Windows-only System.Drawing imaging surface:
      Controllers\RoxyFilemanController.cs   Bitmap, Graphics, PixelFormat,
                                             InterpolationMode, Image.GetThumbnailImageAbort
    Design section 7 resolves that by REWRITING those call sites onto
    SixLabors.ImageSharp at task 8.6 rather than by pinning the whole project to Windows.
    The three ColorTranslator.FromHtml(...) colour-string validations in
    Controllers\ProductController.cs and Controllers\CheckoutAttributeController.cs are
    not an imaging pipeline at all and map onto SixLabors.ImageSharp.Color.TryParse.
    Until 8.6 lands, those five files are part of the CS* error inventory.

    ===================================================================================
    THE STRUCTURAL TRAP, SEEN FROM THIS SIDE
    ===================================================================================
    Nop.Admin does not live beside Nop.Web - it lives INSIDE it, at
    src\Presentation\Nop.Web\Administration\. Task 7.1 had to add
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);Administration\**;Plugins\**</DefaultItemExcludes>
    to Nop.Web.csproj so Nop.Web's implicit globs would not swallow this whole tree.

    The inverse needs NO exclusion, and that was verified by evaluating the resolved item
    lists rather than by reading globs. MSBuild roots the default globs at the directory
    containing the project file, so Nop.Admin's globs cannot reach UP into Nop.Web; and
    nothing is nested inside Administration\. Measured after conversion:
      Compile  277 items  (Models 127, Controllers 71, Validators 61, Extensions 8,
                           Infrastructure 3, AdminAreaRegistration.cs,
                           Properties\AssemblyInfo.cs, ...)   0 leakage
      Content  340 items  (325 .cshtml + 12 .json + 3 .config)  0 leakage
    277 is exactly the length of the legacy explicit <Compile> list, and 325 is exactly
    the .cshtml count on disk, so the globs reproduce the old compilation set precisely -
    no file silently gained or lost. Zero resolved item path escapes this directory.

    One consequence of the legacy layout is gone and is worth naming: the old project set
    <OutputPath>..\bin\</OutputPath>, i.e. it dropped Nop.Admin.dll straight into
    Nop.Web\bin as the MVC "Admin" area. That was a BUILD/DEPLOY relationship, not a
    compile-time reference (design section 6). The SDK writes to
    Administration\bin\Debug\net10.0\ instead, and task 8.2 re-expresses the hosting
    relationship as MapAreaControllerRoute contributed through IRouteProvider.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>

    <!--
      TASK 8.2 - REQUIRED, and its absence would fail the 8.8 gate with a single confusing error
      that has nothing to do with the ~3000 CS*/RZ* porting errors 8.1 inventoried.

      Nop.Admin is a LIBRARY that contributes an MVC area to the Nop.Web host. It is not an
      independently launchable application, has no Program.cs and never had a Global.asax (3.90
      recorded that as UseCustomServer=True with an external CustomServerUrl). But
      Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web defaults OutputType to Exe - MEASURED, not assumed:
      `dotnet msbuild Nop.Admin.csproj -getProperty:OutputType` returns "Exe" with this property
      removed - and an Exe with no Main gets

          CSC : error CS5001: Program does not contain a static 'Main' method suitable for an
          entry point

      which was observed on a throwaway Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web probe of this exact shape.

      NOTE ON WHEN IT BITES, because this was checked and the naive expectation was wrong:
      removing this property today does NOT produce CS5001, because Roslyn never reaches
      entry-point resolution while the compilation still has ~3000 DECLARATION-phase errors -
      the same phase-ordering behaviour recorded in runtime-deferrals.md section 19. The probe
      reproduced both sides: with only a method-BODY error present, CS5001 was reported
      alongside it; with declaration errors present, it was not. So CS5001 would have surfaced
      for the first time exactly when 8.3/8.4 finished and the gate was expected to pass.

      Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web is still the right SDK: it imports the Razor SDK, which is what
      compiles the 325 views under Areas/Admin/Views/.
    -->
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    packages.config -> PackageReference (Requirements 1.3, 1.5).
    src\Presentation\Nop.Web\Administration\packages.config listed 15 packages and IS
    DELETED by this task.

    Versions are governed centrally by src\Directory.Packages.props
    (ManagePackageVersionsCentrally=true, CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled=false), so
    every item below is intentionally version-less. All six already had a <PackageVersion>
    entry from earlier stages, so this task added NO new central pin.

    Only DIRECT dependencies are declared - PackageReference is transitive by default,
    unlike packages.config which enumerated the whole closure. Each of the six was
    verified against actual source usage under Administration\, not assumed:

      Autofac                  Infrastructure\DependencyRegistrar.cs
                               ("using Autofac", "using Autofac.Core":
                               ContainerBuilder, IDependencyRegistrar implementation)
      AutoMapper               Infrastructure\Mapper\AdminMapperConfiguration.cs
                               ("using AutoMapper") plus Extensions\MappingExtensions.cs.
                               NOTE this differs from Nop.Services, which reaches
                               AutoMapper only transitively and therefore declares
                               nothing - here the usage is direct.
      FluentValidation         110 files - every Models\**\*Model.cs carrying
                               [Validator(typeof(...))] from FluentValidation.Attributes,
                               and all 61 Validators\**\*Validator.cs deriving from
                               BaseNopValidator/AbstractValidator<T>. Pinned at 7.6.105;
                               see design section 9 and the long rationale in
                               Directory.Packages.props for why this is a deliberate
                               NON-upgrade. NO FluentValidation.Mvc / .AspNetCore
                               reference is created - task 6.2 hand-wrote the ASP.NET Core
                               integration (NopFluentValidationModelValidatorProvider) and
                               task 6.4 registers it inside AddNopFramework.
      Newtonsoft.Json          Controllers\BaseAdminController.cs
                               ("using Newtonsoft.Json.Converters": IsoDateTimeConverter,
                               fed to Nop.Web.Framework's ConverterJsonResult when
                               AdminAreaSettings.UseIsoDateTimeConverterInJson is on).
                               Task 6.2 deliberately kept ConverterJsonResult on explicit
                               Newtonsoft serialization so its payload stays byte-identical
                               to 3.90 regardless of the host's formatter choice
                               (runtime-deferrals.md section 11.23), so this dependency is
                               load-bearing and not a leftover.
      SixLabors.ImageSharp     NOT referenced by any source file TODAY - declared for
                               TASK 8.6, which rewrites RoxyFilemanController.cs and the
                               ColorTranslator sites (design section 7). Declared here so
                               8.6 does not have to reopen the project file, exactly as
                               task 7.1 declared Autofac.Extensions.DependencyInjection
                               for task 7.2. Pinned at 2.1.13 - the last purely
                               Apache-2.0 line; see the long licence note in
                               Directory.Packages.props before considering 3.x/4.x, and
                               runtime-deferrals.md section 7.18 for the licence
                               diagnostic that affects this project at 8.6.
      System.ServiceModel.Syndication
                               SyndicationFeed / SyndicationItem in
                               Controllers\HomeController.cs (the nopCommerce news feed
                               on the admin dashboard). The net10.0 targeting pack ships
                               System.ServiceModel.Web.dll but NOT
                               System.ServiceModel.Syndication.dll, so the out-of-band
                               Microsoft package is genuinely required - the same finding
                               tasks 6.1 and 7.1 recorded.

    ===================================================================================
    DROPPED - the other 9 packages, by category
    ===================================================================================
    (1) THE System.Web MVC 5 STACK - superseded wholesale by the implicit
        Microsoft.AspNetCore.App FrameworkReference (design section 5):
          Microsoft.AspNet.Mvc 5.2.3          System.Web.Mvc
          Microsoft.AspNet.Razor 3.2.3        System.Web.Razor
          Microsoft.AspNet.WebPages 3.2.3     System.Web.WebPages / .Deployment / .Razor,
                                              System.Web.Helpers
          Microsoft.Web.Infrastructure 1.0.0  DynamicModuleUtility for System.Web
        The matching BCL <Reference> items go with them: System.Web,
        System.Web.Abstractions, System.Web.Routing, System.Web.Extensions,
        System.Web.Services, System.Web.DynamicData, System.Web.Entity,
        System.Web.ApplicationServices, System.EnterpriseServices - none has a net10.0
        counterpart. This is the root cause of the overwhelming majority of the CS*
        errors handed to tasks 8.2/8.3/8.4.

    (2) BUNDLING AND MINIFICATION - DROPPED BY DESIGN, NO SUCCESSOR (design section 8):
          Microsoft.AspNet.Web.Optimization 1.1.3   System.Web.Optimization
          WebGrease 1.6.0                            the minifier it wrapped
          Antlr 3.5.0.2                              WebGrease's CSS parser
        VERIFIED UNUSED HERE, which makes this a free drop rather than a port: a
        recursive search of every .cs and .cshtml under Administration\ finds ZERO
        occurrences of System.Web.Optimization, WebGrease and Antlr. Nop.Admin never
        bound to the bundling API - the whole binding lived in Nop.Web.Framework's
        UI\PageHeadBuilder.cs, which task 6.3 already re-based onto individual tags plus
        IFileVersionProvider cache busting while keeping GenerateScripts /
        GenerateCssFiles signature-compatible. The three packages were present only
        because Web.config's <namespaces> listed System.Web.Optimization for Razor
        IntelliSense and its <runtime> block carried binding redirects for the other two.
        CONSEQUENCE FOR TASK 8.4: the _ViewImports.cshtml that replaces Web.config's
        <namespaces> must NOT carry System.Web.Optimization.
        SEPARATELY, task 8.4 must remove the two now-dead bundling checkboxes from
        Views\Setting\GeneralCommon.cshtml (they bind SeoSettings.EnableJsBundling /
        EnableCssBundling, which survive on the entity - no DB migration - but are inert).
        Design section 8 spells this out.

    (3) net45-ERA POLYFILLS - now framework-provided:
          Microsoft.Bcl 1.1.10, Microsoft.Bcl.Async 1.0.168 (Microsoft.Threading.Tasks
            x3), Microsoft.Bcl.Build 1.0.21, Microsoft.Net.Http 2.2.29
            (System.Net.Http.Extensions, System.Net.Http.Primitives)
        Task/HttpClient polyfills plus the MSBuild targets package that enforced them.
        The Microsoft.Bcl.Build.targets Import and its EnsureNuGetPackageBuildImports
        guard target go with them.

    Likewise the remaining BCL <Reference> items - System, System.Core (implicit),
    System.Data, System.Data.DataSetExtensions, System.Xml, System.Xml.Linq,
    Microsoft.CSharp, System.Net, System.Net.Http, System.Net.Http.WebRequest,
    System.IO.Compression.FileSystem, System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations - all in the
    net10.0 shared framework - plus three that need naming individually:

      System.Drawing         REMOVED per the task brief. Its five call sites are real and
                             are task 8.6's, rewritten onto SixLabors.ImageSharp above.
                             System.Drawing.Common is Windows-only since .NET 6 and would
                             have forced net10.0-windows on this project.
      System.Configuration   REMOVED. It has exactly ONE call site:
                             Controllers\CommonController.cs line 458,
                             ConfigurationManager.GetSection("system.web/machineKey")
                             as MachineKeySection, in the admin System Info page.
                             MachineKeySection does not exist on .NET at all - ASP.NET
                             Core replaced <machineKey> with Data Protection - so this is
                             not a "route it through IConfiguration" migration but a
                             removal. TASK 8.7 owns it. (Note the same file has a second
                             System-Info defect already recorded: runtime-deferrals.md
                             section 2 says lines 205 and 217 must report trust level
                             "Full" unconditionally, because CommonHelper.GetTrustLevel()
                             and AspNetHostingPermissionLevel were deleted in task 2.4.)
      System.ServiceModel    replaced by the System.ServiceModel.Syndication package above.

    NOT NEEDED, checked rather than assumed - a recursive search of Administration\
    finds ZERO occurrences of: ImageResizer, System.Runtime.Caching, System.Data.Entity
    (EF6), OfficeOpenXml (EPPlus), iTextSharp, MaxMind, System.Linq.Dynamic,
    StackExchange (Redis or Profiling), MiniProfiler, Owin, System.Web.Http and
    System.Web.Optimization. So none of the packages dropped project-wide - SQL Server
    Compact (design section 10), MiniProfiler, Autofac.Mvc5, OWIN/Web API 2, ImageResizer -
    has a call site here to port.

    Autofac.Extensions.DependencyInjection is deliberately NOT referenced either.
    Nop.Web declares it because Program.cs calls
    builder.Host.UseServiceProviderFactory(new AutofacServiceProviderFactory()).
    Nop.Admin has no host: its Infrastructure\DependencyRegistrar.cs receives an Autofac
    ContainerBuilder from NopEngine and needs plain Autofac only. Task 6.4 already removed
    the Autofac.Mvc5 plumbing (AutofacDependencyResolver, RequestLifetimeScopeProvider,
    RegisterControllers) from Nop.Web.Framework, and its replacement
    RegisterAssemblyTypes scan over ControllerBase picks up this project's controllers
    from the TypeFinder without any per-project package.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Autofac" />
    <PackageReference Include="AutoMapper" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" />
    <PackageReference Include="SixLabors.ImageSharp" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.ServiceModel.Syndication" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ProjectReferences to the already-migrated SDK-style upstream projects (Requirement 2.4).
    All four had reached their clean-compile gate before this task started: Nop.Core (2.5),
    Nop.Data (3.3), Nop.Services (4.3) and Nop.Web.Framework (6.6).

    The legacy <Project>{GUID}</Project> / <Name>...</Name> metadata children are dropped -
    the SDK resolves references by path:
      {6BDA8332-939F-45B7-A25E-7A797260AE59} Nop.Core
      {ccd7f2c9-6a2c-4cf0-8e89-076b8fc0f144} Nop.Data
      {210541AD-F659-47DA-8763-16F36C5CD2F4} Nop.Services
      {75FD4163-333C-4DD5-854D-2EF294E45D94} Nop.Web.Framework

    NO reference to Nop.Web is added, and none was removed: the legacy project file had
    exactly these four. Nop.Web and Nop.Admin are siblings over Nop.Web.Framework
    (design section 6), and adding an edge in either direction would be a design change,
    not a migration. If task 8.2 decides the Nop.Web host must load this assembly, that
    is a hosting/discovery decision for 8.2 to make and document.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Libraries\Nop.Core\Nop.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Libraries\Nop.Data\Nop.Data.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Libraries\Nop.Services\Nop.Services.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Nop.Web.Framework\Nop.Web.Framework.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================
    PUBLISH SHAPING - TASK 8.1, RUNTIME DEFERRAL 7.4-2. SECURITY RELEVANT.
    ===================================================================================
    3.90 shaped its publish output from Nop.Web.csproj, whose ExcludeFilesFromDeployment
    list included TWO entries that belong to THIS tree:

        Administration\bin\**
        Administration\db_backups\*.bak     <- DATABASE BACKUPS

    Task 7.4 could not re-express either of them, because Administration\** is in
    Nop.Web.csproj's DefaultItemExcludes, so nothing under this directory is an item of
    that project. It recorded the gap as deferral 7.4-2 and assigned it here.

    Administration\bin\** needs no entry - the SDK never publishes intermediate output,
    and the flat 3.90 ..\bin\ OutputPath is gone.

    db_backups is the security-relevant one and is handled below.

    Both directions are needed, for the same reason task 7.4 discovered on the storefront
    side (runtime-deferrals.md section 31.4): the legacy project used ~1200 explicit
    <Content Include=.../> entries to copy Content\ and Scripts\ into the output, but the
    SDK classifies .js/.css/.png/.gif/.woff/.map/.svg/.eot/.ttf/.otf/.swf as `None`, and
    `None` items default to CopyToPublishDirectory=Never. WITHOUT the first ItemGroup
    below a published Nop.Admin would have NO admin CSS, NO admin JavaScript, NO AdminLTE
    or bootstrap assets, NO TinyMCE and NO Roxy Fileman - i.e. an entirely unstyled,
    non-functional admin UI. That is a functional regression, not untidiness, and it is
    not something deferral 7.4-2 called out.

    `Update` is used rather than `Include` throughout: every one of these files is ALREADY
    an item from implicit globbing. `Include` would produce duplicates and fail the build
    with NETSDK1022; `Update` only changes metadata, and no-ops harmlessly for a path that
    does not exist.

    INTERACTION WITH TASK 8.5, which owns admin static assets: 8.5 decides WHERE the
    assets are SERVED from - either by relocating Content\ and Scripts\ under wwwroot, or
    (cheaper, and what deferral 7.4-2 recommends) by widening
    Nop.Web\Infrastructure\NopStaticFileProvider.cs's allow-list with
    Administration/Content/** and Administration/Scripts/**. That is orthogonal to
    WHETHER the files are DEPLOYED, which is what this block fixes. If 8.5 relocates the
    trees under wwwroot, wwwroot publishes by default and these two entries become
    harmless no-ops.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      (a) Runtime assets that MUST reach the publish output.

      Content\  - the admin stylesheets and images (adminLTE, bootstrap, kendo, TinyMCE,
                  Roxy Fileman, fonts) plus Content\Roxy_Fileman\conf.json, which
                  RoxyFilemanController READS at runtime (Configuration()/GetSetting()).
      Scripts\  - the admin JavaScript (kendo, fineuploader, tagEditor, typeahead, ...).
      db_backups\ - for the placeholder ONLY; the .bak files are withheld immediately
                  below. This entry is NOT redundant and it is NOT cosmetic - see the
                  measured defect note in the withheld block.
      sitemap.config is NOT listed: it is a .config, so the SDK already classifies it as
      Content, and Content defaults to CopyToPublishDirectory=PreserveNewest. It must
      publish - Views\Shared\Menu.cshtml line 7 does
      siteMap.LoadFrom("~/Administration/sitemap.config"), so the admin menu does not
      render without it. Verified present in the computed publish set.
    -->
    <None Update="Content\**" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <None Update="Scripts\**" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <None Update="db_backups\**" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      (b) Withheld. These Update items come AFTER the ones above, so they win.

      *** THE SECURITY ITEM: DATABASE BACKUPS. ***
      IMaintenanceService.BackupDatabase() writes SQL Server .bak files to
      Nop.Services\Common\MaintenanceService.GetBackupDirectoryPath(), which is
      CommonHelper.MapPath("~/") + "Administration" + "db_backups" - i.e. THIS directory,
      resolved against the HOST's content root. A .bak is a full copy of the store's
      database. Publishing one into a web-served directory is a data-exposure bug, which
      is why 3.90 withheld it and why deferral 7.4-2 is tagged security-relevant.

      NOTE THE PATTERN IS `db_backups\**\*.bak`, NOT `db_backups\**`.
      Deferral 7.4-2 and tasks.md both suggest the broader `db_backups\**`, and that
      would be WRONG - it would also withhold db_backups\placeholder.txt, and
      MaintenanceService.GetAllBackupFiles() opens with

          if (!System.IO.Directory.Exists(path)) throw new IOException("Backup directory not exists");

      so an empty (hence absent) directory makes the admin Maintenance page THROW rather
      than show "no backups". The legacy project shipped the placeholder via
      <Content Include="db_backups\placeholder.txt" /> for exactly this reason, and
      .gitignore line 174 is already correctly narrowed to
      `src/Presentation/Nop.Web/Administration/db_backups/*.bak`, so the placeholder is
      tracked. This is the same trap task 7.4 hit and fixed for
      Content\Images\Thumbs\placeholder.txt (runtime-deferrals.md section 31.3).

      *** MEASURED DEFECT, FOUND AND FIXED WITHIN THIS TASK - DO NOT "SIMPLIFY" THIS. ***
      Narrowing the exclusion to *.bak is necessary but NOT sufficient, and assuming it
      was would have shipped a broken Maintenance page. placeholder.txt is a .txt, so the
      SDK classifies it as `None`, and `None` defaults to CopyToPublishDirectory=Never -
      i.e. under the SDK the placeholder is withheld BY DEFAULT even with no exclusion at
      all. The first computed publish set for this project confirmed it: placeholder.txt
      absent. That is why `db_backups\**` appears in the INCLUDE group above as well; the
      two entries are ordered so PreserveNewest lands first and Never wins for *.bak.
      Verified afterwards with a planted .bak: placeholder.txt PRESENT, .bak ABSENT - a
      discriminating result, where testing the exclusion alone could not distinguish
      "my rule works" from "None defaults to Never anyway".

      ALSO NOT REACHABLE OVER HTTP, and that is a deliberate 7.4 decision with a
      consequence for task 8.x: 3.90's Nop.Web\Web.config carried
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".bak" mimeType="application/octet-stream"/> with the comment
      "Allow database backup (.bak) file loading", i.e. backups were downloadable as
      static files - and Controllers\CommonController.cs line 584 still builds exactly
      such a link (GetStoreLocation() + "Administration/db_backups/" + p.Name). Task 7.4
      deliberately did not reproduce the mimeMap (runtime-deferrals.md section 33.2), and
      NopStaticFileProvider additionally denies the .bak extension and excludes
      Administration\ from its allow-list. So that download link is dead by design. TASK
      8.3/8.5 must replace it with a controller action that streams the file under the
      existing admin permission check - which is the correct design anyway, and must NOT
      be "fixed" by re-adding the mimeMap or widening the static-file allow-list.
    -->
    <None Update="db_backups\**\*.bak" CopyToPublishDirectory="Never" />

    <!--
      Legacy Razor/MVC5 configuration files. Both are DELETED by later tasks - Web.config
      by 8.7, Views\Web.config by 8.4 - and neither can be read by anything on .NET 10:
      Web.config's own comment says "We use this file to make razor intellisense work in
      this project", and its content is a System.Web <pages><namespaces> list (System.Web.Mvc,
      System.Web.Optimization, ...), a <compilation targetFramework="4.5.1"/>, five
      System.Web/OWIN appSettings keys and a <runtime><assemblyBinding> block of
      .NET Framework binding redirects. Views\Web.config declares a
      System.Web.WebPages.Razor <configSections> group.

      They are excluded from PUBLISH here as the same interim measure task 7.4 applied to
      Nop.Web's two view Web.config files, for two concrete reasons:
        1. IIS parses every file named web.config in the served tree (case-insensitively
           on Windows), and a <configSections> group naming an assembly that does not
           exist on .NET 10 produces HTTP 500.19.
        2. The SDK's TransformWebConfig task matches the LITERAL lowercase filename
           web.config, which "Web.config" is not on a case-sensitive filesystem - task
           7.4 measured exactly this and renamed Nop.Web's file to fix it. So a publish
           of this project emits its own generated lowercase web.config alongside the
           legacy capital-W one: two files differing only in case, which is legal on
           Linux and a collision on Windows. Excluding the legacy one avoids it.
      Once 8.4/8.7 delete the files these two entries become no-ops and can go, exactly as
      task 7.5 removed 7.4's equivalents from Nop.Web.csproj.
    -->
    <Content Update="Web.config" CopyToPublishDirectory="Never" />
    <!--
      TASK 8.4 DELETED Areas\Admin\Views\Web.config, so its interim
      <Content Update="Areas\Admin\Views\Web.config" CopyToPublishDirectory="Never" /> entry has
      been REMOVED from this group rather than left behind as a no-op - exactly as task 7.5
      removed 7.4's two equivalents from Nop.Web.csproj. It is superseded by
      Areas\Admin\Views\_ViewImports.cshtml, which carries the pageBaseType (as @inherits) and the
      <namespaces> list (as @using) that were the only two things in it .NET 10 could act on.
      The Web.config entry above survives until task 8.7 deletes that file.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
